// M3g — Mixed CPU+I/O Overlap (poll-path fusion)
//
// M3g ships no new compile-error classes. Fusing an independent CPU call with an
// independent I/O call into one shared continuation is fully automatic, exactly
// like M3b (I/O+I/O overlap) and M3d (CPU+CPU overlap): the compiler decides what
// fuses and what runs sequentially. There is no opt-in syntax a user can write
// incorrectly, and every shape the fusion cannot safely admit (a nested mixed
// group, a param-taking host, a wide-value-`errors` CPU-shaped call, a
// guard-tripping crossing, ...) declines SILENTLY to the same sequential lowering
// `--no-auto-parallel` already produces — no diagnostic fires, because nothing
// is wrong; the program is still correct, just not fused.
//
// The one existing diagnostic that IS relevant here, unchanged in mechanism but
// widened in scope by this milestone: `wait` on a purely CPU-bound callee (the
// WaitOnNonMayBlockWarning introduced in M2, already updated for M3d's CPU+CPU
// groups) is ALSO an ordering barrier against a mixed CPU+I/O group. A function
// like
//
//   function crunch(n: int) -> int { ... loop, no suspension ... }
//   function fetchReport() -> string { wait sleep(60); return `...` }
//   function example() -> nothing {
//     let a = wait crunch(3)   // <- WARNING: wait has no effect here
//     let b = fetchReport()
//     print(a)
//     print(b)
//   }
//
// silently defeats the mixed-group fusion this milestone adds: `crunch` never
// suspends, so `wait crunch(3)` triggers WaitOnNonMayBlockWarning (its WHY-text
// already generically names "ends any parallel group that `crunch` could have
// joined" — mixed groups included, not just pure-CPU ones) AND the explicit
// `wait` statement itself is what the fused-group classifier treats as an
// ordering barrier (`admitted_fused_group` never admits a member with an
// explicit `wait` at its own call site) — removing the `wait` is both the fix
// for the warning and what lets the compiler consider fusing `crunch` with an
// adjacent independent I/O call. This is the same diagnostic, the same fix, and
// the same underlying mechanism M3d's gallery documents for pure-CPU groups —
// no new template, no new wording, only a wider set of shapes it now teaches
// against.
//
// See docs/internal/implementation/IMP-concurrency.md "CPU Statement
// Parallelization (M3d)" and its M3g mixed-group amendments for the full
// promotion/decline rules.
